WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.117
Post-quantum migration has real engineering consequences.

2
00:00:04.117 --> 00:00:11.084
Some algorithms use larger keys, larger signatures, or larger messages than teams are used to handling.

3
00:00:11.084 --> 00:00:22.484
That can affect bandwidth, storage, packet sizes, certificate chains, handshake behavior, logging volume, database columns, message queues, and embedded protocols.

4
00:00:22.484 --> 00:00:31.193
Even when the cryptography is correct, surrounding systems may assume older sizes or timing patterns and fail in unexpected ways.

5
00:00:31.193 --> 00:00:35.389
Performance must be measured in the environment that matters.

6
00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:49.877
A developer workstation benchmark is useful, but it is not the same as a busy API gateway, a constrained device, a high-volume signing service, a hardware-backed key operation, or a remote site with limited bandwidth.

7
00:00:49.877 --> 00:01:00.882
Teams should test latency, CPU, memory, concurrency, certificate validation paths, key generation, signing throughput, and failure modes under realistic load.

8
00:01:00.882 --> 00:01:04.603
Legacy and embedded systems are often the hardest part.

9
00:01:04.603 --> 00:01:14.816
Industrial devices, network appliances, fielded products, long-lived firmware, and older operating systems may not support new algorithms quickly.

10
00:01:14.816 --> 00:01:29.067
Cloud and SaaS dependencies introduce another layer: customers may have to wait for provider support, review roadmap claims, update integrations, and verify that configuration options are actually available.

11
00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:34.133
Procurement and vendor management become part of the technical migration.

12
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:37.617
Operations teams also need updated observability.

13
00:01:37.617 --> 00:01:50.205
Monitoring, alerts, asset inventory, vulnerability management, configuration review, and incident response playbooks may need to understand new protocol options and failure states.

14
00:01:50.205 --> 00:01:58.914
A strong program involves architecture, engineering, security, operations, procurement, compliance, and vendor management.

15
00:01:58.914 --> 00:02:08.098
PQC adoption is not just choosing an algorithm; it is making sure the whole system can run, be monitored, and be supported after the change.
